---
title: "Reading settings"
---

This page is a reading surface. The **Settings** gear (top-right of the built page) lets a
reader pick a theme (light, dark, or sepia). The choice is stored in the reader's own browser
and applied before the next page paints, so it never flashes and never touches the author's
source.

## Why a reader theme

A document is read far more often than it is written, on screens the author never saw: a
phone in bright sun, a wide monitor at night, a room lit only by the screen. Reader settings
move that last decision, *how the page is lit*, from the author to the reader, where it
belongs: a bright, a dark, or a warm sepia page, chosen per reader.

The text below is here to look right in every theme.

## A paragraph of running prose

The gap between a comfortable read and an uncomfortable one is small and entirely mechanical:
the size of the type, the length of the line, and the contrast of the page. Set the measure
too wide and the eye loses the start of the next line; too narrow and the rhythm breaks every
few words. Sepia exists because a warm, low-contrast page is easier on the eye in dim light
than stark black on white. None of this is decoration. It is the difference between finishing
the chapter and closing the tab.

## A figure

![A calm reading column.](../media/fit-a.png){#fig-reading}

@fig-reading should keep its caption and numbering in every theme.

## Some code and a little math

```python
def comfortable(width_ch: int) -> bool:
    return 45 <= width_ch <= 75
```

Inline math like $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ and a display equation should both stay crisp in every
theme:

$$ \text{measure} \approx \frac{\text{line length}}{\text{character width}} $$

## A callout

::: {.callout-note}
The reader's theme choice is reader-local and read-only. It never writes back to the `.tmd`,
adds no output format, and ships offline, so it sits cleanly inside Taliesin's invariants.
:::
